BabAR License v1.0 (inherited from BabyHuBERT)

This license (the “License”) between you (“the Licensee”) and Maxime Poli, Alejandrina Cristia, Emmanuel Dupoux, and Marvin Lavechin ("Licensors") is designed with a dual purpose: to enable scientific progress and open access to models trained on data from under-represented communities, while ensuring that such access does not come at the cost of exploiting or harming those same communities.

Section I: PREAMBLE

The lack of freely-available, pre-trained Natural Language Processing (“NLP”) models in the majority of the world’s languages is concerning. Expanding linguistic diversity in mainstream NLP research is necessary,  yet releasing open-access models and datasets representing under-represented populations, including Indigenous Peoples, can backfire into exploitation of or discrimination against those same communities (as outlined in Articles 2; 13–16; and 31 of the United Nations Declaration on the Rights of Indigenous People).
Rather than restricting access entirely, this License seeks a middle path: one that promotes interdisciplinary collaborations between speech technologists, linguistics, and the communities whose data underlies this research. Drawing on the Responsible AI License (RAIL)-M framework, the Licensors  have adapted it to apply to under-resourced languages and, specifically, to the BabyHuBERT model (defined in §1.4 below). The Licensors view this License as an opportunity for the research community to work in ways that are inclusive and respectful of the world's children and their families, and have modified the language accordingly to improve accessibility.

This License governs two categories of users. Developers (those who use the Model to build derivative models or applications) must abide by this License and ensure that any publicly released Derivative inherits, at minimum, the same use-based restrictions. End users (those who simply use the Outputs of a Derivative, e.g., a researcher running a voice type classifier fine-tuned from the Model) are not required to sign or abide by this License, as Outputs (defined in $1.3) are not governed by it. 
Now therefore, the Licensee and the Licensors agree as follows:

1. Definitions

"License" means the terms and conditions for use, reproduction, and distribution as defined in this document.
"Data" means the child-centered multilingual speech recordings used to train BabyHuBERT. The Data is not licensed under this License.
“Output” is the result of operating the Model or its derivatives; e.g. the output files resulting from applying a voice type classifier.
“Model” means BabyHuBERT, a self-supervised speech model consisting of a neural network architecture and its associated trained parameters (weights and checkpoints), pre-trained on child-centered multilingual speech data: see Charlot, T., Kunze, T., Poli, M., Cristia, A., Dupoux, E., & Lavechin, M. (2026). BabyHuBERT: Multilingual self-supervised learning for segmenting speakers in child-centered long-form recordings. arXiv. https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2509.15001.
“Derivatives of the Model” means all modifications to the Model, which is created by initializing from BabyHuBERT parameters, or trained using BabyHubert activations or output, whether BabyHubert is used alone or as a component of a larger system.
“Complementary Material” means the accompanying source code and scripts used to define, run, load, benchmark or evaluate the Model, and used to prepare data for training or evaluation, if any. This includes any accompanying documentation, tutorials, examples, if any.
“Distribution” means any transmission, reproduction, publication or other sharing of Derivatives of the Model to a third party, including providing the Model as a hosted service made available by electronic or other remote means, e.g. API-based or web access.
“Licensors” means Maxime Poli, Emmanuel Dupoux, Alejandrina Cristia, and Marvin Lavechin, acting in the capacity as legal administrator of this License. The Licensors hold the rights necessary to grant this License and to take legal action against any Licensee who violates its terms.
“Licensee” means an individual or Legal Entity exercising permissions granted by this License, including through the use of the Model or a Derivative thereof - e.g. for voice type classification, addressee detection, or speech transcription.
“Ethics Discussion Body” means the community body currently operating as DARCLE, convened through a regular meeting cycle, maintaining an elected leadership board, and reachable through the mailing list (darcle@googlegroups.com). 
“Third Parties” means individuals or legal entities that are not under the control of the Licensors or the Licensee.
“Commercial Use” means any use of the Model, Derivatives of the Model and Complementary Material primarily intended for or directed toward commercial advantage or monetary compensation.
“Natural Language” means spoken, signed, or written communication as used by a community of people in their daily life and for their own purposes.
“Natural Language Processing” (or “NLP”) means algorithms that allow computers to process language automatically (written, spoken, or signed)

