Attachment A

Use Restrictions

The Licensee agrees not to use the Model or Derivatives of the Model:

A. For any commercial purposes primarily intended for or directed toward commercial advantage or monetary compensation, with the exception of applications incorporating benefit-sharing (see below);
B. In any way that violates any applicable national, federal, state, local or international law or regulation;
C. For fully automated decision making that adversely impacts an individual’s legal rights or otherwise creates or modifies a binding, enforceable obligation;

D. Regarding Derivatives with generative capabilities: 
a. To generate or disseminate false information and/or content;
b. To generate or disseminate personal identifiable information that can be used to harm an individual;
c. To impersonate or attempt to impersonate (e.g. deepfakes) others without their consent;
E. For harmful purposes, for example exploitation, defamation, disparagement, discrimination, or surveillance.

The potential for harm emerging from some applications may depend on contextual factors. For any application where you are uncertain of whether its end uses may entail harm, we recommend sharing your general research questions or expected purposes ahead of time with the Ethics Discussion Body  (darcle@googlegroups.com), or its leadership, if needing to preserve confidentiality, so as to invite discussion on it and get informed by the community. 

We ask Licensees to email the Ethics Discussion Body  (darcle@googlegroups.com), or its leadership, if needing to preserve confidentiality, ideally in advance of publication

A. For any commercial purposes which contain a benefit-sharing approach. For advice and concrete examples of benefit sharing, see https://nagoyaprotocol-hub.de/benefit-sharing/ and the InfoSheet 2024 on “Approaches to Benefit Sharing” available from https://www.waikato.ac.nz/assets/Uploads/Research/Research-institutes-centres-and-groups/Institutes/TKRI/Information-sheets/TKRI-IP-Infosheet-Approaches-23-Sep-2024-Online.pdf.
B. Since the Model was neither designed nor validated for clinical use, its application in this domain may introduce biases and increase the risk of misdiagnosis, in cases like:
a. Research on predictive profiling of children, such as attempts to forecast educational outcomes based on speech development;
b. Clinical decision-making applications, including the diagnosis of speech pathologies; 
c. Neurodivergent related applications constitute another example of harmful but non malicious derivative application. In this scenario, downstream researchers would fine-tune BabyHuBERT for neurodevelopmental screening or intervention. They could identify neurodivergent speech patterns as disorders and recommend interventions to eliminate these patterns rather than accommodate them. They could also proceed without input from the neurodivergent advocacy organisations who should be consulted for such research.
d. To monitor children's speech for content-based screening, behavioral profiling, or enforcement purposes.

Attachment B

Known risks

We note that the Licensors do not consider it technically possible to reconstruct the original training data from the Model. They invite anyone with evidence contradicting this belief to communicate it to the Ethical Discussion Board.

Risk 1 – Temporal Consent Gap

The audio recordings used to pre-train BabyHuBERT were collected in the context of child language research and before machine-learning techniques like “pre-training”, “self-supervision” or “representation learning” existed; moreover, recent developments have led the general population to hold ideas about AI-based modelling, which was an arcane topic before. Neither the researchers who collected the data nor the families who agreed to participate could have anticipated this type of use — and the children recorded cannot retroactively consent now that they have grown up. As a result, any use of BabyHuBERT or its derivative inherits this gap between what participants originally consented to and what their data ultimately enabled.

Risk 2 – Uncertainty of Scope of Reuse

Since BabyHuBERT is a speech representation model, it can be used for many things. It is that much harder to define which reuses are aligned with the original consent form, and which may ultimately prove to be harmful. Furthermore, by increasing the accessibility of BabyHuBERT, derivative models increase this risk, because they can extend it to new audiences and new applications beyond what had been anticipated.

Risk 3 – Diffusion of Moral Responsibility

BabyHuBERT exists within a chain of decisions — data collection, pre-training, fine-tuning, deployment — each made by different actors at different times, with each actor believing that a legal document (a consent form, a license) can protect them from responsibility. This creates a temptation to locate responsibility elsewhere: model creators may assume the data comes without ethical baggage, and end-users may assume that a publicly available model has been vetted for their own use case. In the current fast-moving AI world, however, every actor in this chain shares the ethical responsibilities that arise. The parent who signed the consent form cannot control what the end user of a derived model does – instead, each actor in the chain can invite the next to approach the data and models with respect and care. 

Risk 4 – Inequitable Benefit Distribution

Like many NLP models in the past, BabyHuBERT was created by researchers based in the Global North. BabyHuBERT’s creators made a conscious choice to include under-represented communities – but this opened the path to injustice because (a) benefits from BabyHuBERT and its derivative models may not flow back to participants (or the communities they belong to), and (b) BabyHuBERT and its derivative models may benefit certain communities more than others. We strongly recommend to creators of derivative models that they reflect on who can use their derived model and who benefits from their derived model. We also invite users of derivative models to reflect on who may benefit from their own use.

Risk 5 – Challenges to Indigenous Data Sovereignty

The temporal consent gap highlighted the idea that participating families may not have fully foreseen what their data would enable. The present risk serves to highlight an analogue issue for under-represented communities, including indigenous peoples. The data used to create BabyHuBERT included data from such diverse communities; but none of BabyHuBERT’s creators belonged to any of those communities. Licensees should not assume that the model's release implies those communities' endorsement of downstream uses.

Risk 6 – Erosion of trust with respect to researchers

The audio recordings used to pre-train BabyHuBERT were not simply downloaded from a repository — they were collected by researchers who spent years building relationships of trust with communities, 
navigating local ethical expectations, and securing the approvals that made participation possible. These relationships are fragile and ongoing. A downstream use of BabyHuBERT that a community finds objectionable can damage or destroy those relationships, with consequences for the original researchers' ability to continue their work, and also for others seeking to do similar research. Licensees should be aware that their own work (a fine-tuned model, a high-visibility application that draws public attention) carries consequences for other actors in the same environment. 

Risk 7 – Algorithmic Bias

BabyHuBERT was trained on a dataset in which 57% of recordings come from English-speaking, WEIRD (Western, Educated, Industrialized, Rich, Democratic) populations. Its representations may reflect skews in training data, leading to higher performance for some populations and/or languages. This could be compounded by skewed properties of additional data used to generate derivative Models.

Attachment C

Risk mitigation strategies

This attachment describes requirements and recommendations for creators of derivatives, and not end users, that is, people who use models’ outputs only. 

Part 1 — What you are required to do

1. Join the mailing list and describe what you have created

When you create a derivative, we ask you to:
Have at least two co-authors or contributors of that outcome join the mailing list (darcle@googlegroups.com). If you are the sole author, identify someone who can join with you, to decrease the chances that you miss important information.
Write a message to the mailing list with a description of what you have created, including #babyhubert in the subject line. Note that some derivatives may not be released as re-usable models, for example: BabyHuBERT features feeding a one-shot classifier on a single small dataset, or fine-tuned to a specific family’s recordings, or applied to a task where you see poor results. We ask you to inform the community of such derivatives regardless of whether you release the underlying code or weights. If you notify the community well before final stable publication (e.g. in a conference proceeding or journal), you will be able to integrate any feedback you get in replies to your message.
By joining the mailing list, you can benefit from group wisdom; by sharing what you have done, you contribute to a digital log, which will make it easier for everyone to keep track of e.g. potential uses, and provide arguments and evidence regarding which uses could be harmful or inconsistent with this License. 

2. Utilize the mailing list

Pay attention to arguments and evidence brought forward in replies to your message. The community is diverse and they may not all agree. Use your best judgment when integrating their feedback.
Consider searching the mailing list’s history for #babyhubert, to see if others have shared relevant information in the past.
Monitor the list for updates, feedback, or safeguard recommendations. 
Adopt recommended safeguards whenever relevant to your project.

3. Report problems promptly

Report to the mailing list (darcle@googlegroups.com) or its elected board — as soon as you become aware — any of the following:
Unanticipated uses of BabyHuBERT or its derivatives
Ethical concerns
Misuse by third parties

4. Inform users of your Derivative (applies to those releasing models)

If you are releasing a derivative that may, in its stead, have derivatives, make sure to adapt the license or agreement governing your derivative to the conditions in the BabyHuBERT license (saliently paragraph 5 and Attachments A-C).
If you are releasing a derivative intended purely for end-users, we invite you to mention ethical issues you consider relevant to your model.

5. Adopt a reflexive, thoughtful and long-term approach to ethical issues

Try to find relevant ethics training and hold discussions with mentors, peers, and supervisees, to increase the depth of your understanding of the ethical risks and proposed mitigation strategies. We invite you also to adapt extant ethics training to use it with your students, supervisees, and colleagues, as relevant.

Part 2 — What you are encouraged to do

Community engagement

If your derivative focuses on a specific community (e.g., low-SES populations, neurodivergent individuals), consult relevant advocacy organisations as soon as possible.
As relevant to the derivative you are developing, consider seeking input from former participants, community advisory boards, or community representatives (e.g., Tsimane advisory council, Timor-Leste representatives). At present, this recommendation doesn’t appear immediately actionable to us since there isn’t a list of contacts, and it may have negative effects - e.g., many people reaching out to the Tsimane advisory council and taking up their time, or talking to a specific representative of Timor Leste (e.g. an ambassador) who has no idea about the context of BabyHubert, thus causing a potential diplomatic conflict. Consider asking the mailing list for advice if uncertain. 
If there is discussion on how to improve consent forms and explanatory materials — both for future data collection and for communicating past research to relevant communities – consider volunteering to help.
If you are in a position to do so, join efforts to update Attachments A–C as the landscape of use cases and best practices evolves.

Help address inequitable benefit sharing

If you are based in or working with communities in the Global South, or affiliated with communities that contributed data, consider connecting with the Ethics Discussion Board to ask for resource-sharing, support, mentoring. 
Regardless of where they are based, all licensees are invited to volunteer to help with resource-sharing, support, and mentoring.

Technical safeguards

If your derivative targets a demographic not well represented in BabyHuBERT's training data, consider retraining or fine-tuning with speech from that demographic
Conduct privacy auditing: test whether BabyHuBERT or your derivative model is vulnerable to inference or reconstruction attacks, and document the results
Reflect on whether your design choices (compute requirements, assumed acoustic environments, documentation language) inadvertently exclude the communities whose data made this model possible
Questions about any of these obligations or recommendations can be directed to the Ethics Discussion Board via the mailing list (darcle@googlegroups.com).

